## 13:30 Carry the Explanation Forward

We can now answer the central question. The unchanged starting text is the source. Inspection reveals its marks. A code point identifies one character in today’s examples. Normalization applies a chosen Unicode rule. Text preparation combines that rule with chosen changes to lines and whitespace.

Identification tells us what is present. Preparation applies our decisions about what to change. We inspected first, chose NFKC with its limit in view, and traced every step to the result.

This order prevents a common mistake: seeing a difference and assuming it should disappear. A difference can be real, while the purpose still determines whether we preserve or change it. Inspection gives facts; preparation applies chosen rules.

With new text, start the same way. Reveal its marks, identify relevant differences, choose rules that fit the purpose, and test changed starting text. Prepared text is now a dependable building block for the next lesson.
